(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-axial-skeleton-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"The Axial Skeleton — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 3","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How many bones make up the axial skeleton in the adult?","choices":["126 bones","206 bones","22 bones","80 bones"],"answer":3,"exp":"The axial skeleton contains 80 bones — the skull, vertebral column, and thoracic cage along the body's central axis. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which suture joins the two parietal bones along the top midline of the skull?","choices":["The coronal suture","The lambdoid suture","The sagittal suture","The squamous suture"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sagittal suture runs along the superior midline, uniting the right and left parietal bones. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which suture separates the frontal bone from the two parietal bones?","choices":["The coronal suture","The sagittal suture","The lambdoid suture","The squamous suture"],"answer":0,"exp":"The coronal suture crosses the top of the skull, separating the anterior frontal bone from the parietal bones behind it. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which large opening in the occipital bone transmits the spinal cord as it joins the brainstem?","choices":["The optic canal","The jugular foramen","The mental foramen","The foramen magnum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The foramen magnum is the large opening in the occipital bone through which the spinal cord passes to join the brainstem. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which paired projections on the occipital bone articulate with the first cervical vertebra?","choices":["The mastoid processes","The styloid processes","The occipital condyles","The pterygoid processes"],"answer":2,"exp":"The occipital condyles flank the foramen magnum and articulate with the superior facets of the atlas (C1). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which smooth elevation of the frontal bone lies between the eyebrows above the nose?","choices":["The glabella prominence","The mastoid process","The mental protuberance","The occipital crest"],"answer":0,"exp":"The glabella is the smooth prominence of the frontal bone in the midline between the superciliary arches, just above the nose. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Through which opening of the frontal bone do vessels and a nerve reach the forehead above the eye?","choices":["The infraorbital foramen","The stylomastoid foramen","The greater palatine foramen","The supraorbital foramen"],"answer":3,"exp":"The supraorbital foramen (or notch) transmits the supraorbital vessels and nerve onto the forehead above the orbit. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which bony projection of the temporal bone lies just behind the ear and can be felt as a hard bump?","choices":["The coronoid process","The mastoid process","The pterygoid process","The odontoid process"],"answer":1,"exp":"The mastoid process is the rounded projection of the temporal bone behind the ear that serves as a muscle-attachment site. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which slender temporal-bone spike serves as an attachment point for muscles of the tongue and pharynx?","choices":["The mastoid process","The styloid process","The zygomatic arch","The crista galli"],"answer":1,"exp":"The styloid process is the thin pointed projection of the temporal bone that anchors several muscles and ligaments of the tongue and pharynx. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"The external acoustic meatus, the opening of the ear canal, is found in which bone?","choices":["The occipital bone","The temporal bone","The sphenoid bone","The maxilla"],"answer":1,"exp":"The external acoustic meatus, the bony opening of the ear canal, is located in the temporal bone. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which saddle-shaped depression of the sphenoid bone houses the pituitary gland?","choices":["The sella turcica","The cribriform plate","The mandibular fossa","The pterygoid fossa"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sella turcica is the saddle-shaped depression on the body of the sphenoid that cradles the pituitary gland. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Through which sphenoid opening does the optic nerve pass toward the eye?","choices":["The optic canal","The foramen magnum","The jugular foramen","The mastoid foramen"],"answer":0,"exp":"The optic canal in the lesser wing of the sphenoid transmits the optic nerve from the cranial cavity toward the orbit. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which perforated ethmoid plate transmits the olfactory nerve fibers from the nasal cavity?","choices":["The perpendicular plate","The pterygoid plate","The cribriform plate","The horizontal plate"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cribriform plate of the ethmoid is perforated by numerous foramina that transmit olfactory nerve fibers from the nasal cavity into the cranium. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which vertical ethmoid ridge projects into the cranial cavity as an attachment for the falx cerebri?","choices":["The sella turcica","The mastoid process","The crista galli","The mental spine"],"answer":2,"exp":"The crista galli is the upward ethmoid ridge that projects into the cranial cavity and anchors the falx cerebri. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"The maxillae form which part of the mouth and hold which set of teeth?","choices":["The lower jaw, holding the lower teeth","The hard palate only, holding no teeth","The cheekbones, holding no teeth","The upper jaw, holding the upper teeth"],"answer":3,"exp":"The maxillae fuse to form the upper jaw and hold the upper (maxillary) teeth in their alveolar processes. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"The palatine processes of the maxillae form the anterior part of which structure?","choices":["The nasal septum","The orbital floor rim","The hard palate","The zygomatic arch"],"answer":2,"exp":"The palatine processes of the maxillae join in the midline to form the anterior two-thirds of the hard palate (roof of the mouth). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which feature makes the mandible unique among the skull bones?","choices":["It is the only bone with no sutures","It is the only freely movable skull bone","It is the only bone lacking any teeth","It is the only paired facial bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"The mandible is the only freely movable bone of the skull, articulating at the temporomandibular joints. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which mandibular process forms the movable part of the temporomandibular joint?","choices":["The coronoid process","The alveolar process","The mental protuberance","The condylar process"],"answer":3,"exp":"The condylar process of the mandibular ramus bears the head that articulates with the temporal bone at the temporomandibular joint. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Through which mandibular opening do vessels and a nerve emerge onto the chin region?","choices":["The supraorbital foramen","The mental foramen","The foramen ovale","The stylomastoid foramen"],"answer":1,"exp":"The mental foramen on the anterolateral body of the mandible transmits the mental vessels and nerve to the chin and lower lip. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which single bone of the orbit also contains the sella turcica and forms much of the cranial base?","choices":["The lacrimal bone","The sphenoid","The nasal bone","The vomer"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sphenoid contributes to the posterior orbit and, at the cranial base, contains the sella turcica for the pituitary gland. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which unpaired facial bone forms the inferior and posterior part of the bony nasal septum?","choices":["The vomer bone","The nasal bone","The lacrimal bone","The zygomatic bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vomer is the thin, plow-shaped bone that forms the inferoposterior part of the bony nasal septum. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is unusual about the articulations of the hyoid bone?","choices":["It articulates with every cervical vertebra","It fuses with the mandible in adulthood","It articulates directly with the sternum","It does not directly articulate with any other bone"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hyoid is unique in that it does not directly articulate with any other bone, being suspended by muscles and ligaments in the neck. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which fontanelle of the infant skull is the largest and generally the last to close?","choices":["The posterior fontanelle","The mastoid fontanelle","The sphenoidal fontanelle","The anterior fontanelle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior fontanelle is the largest soft spot of the infant skull and typically closes latest, around 18 to 24 months. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which structural unit forms the basic repeating cylinder of compact bone?","choices":["The trabecula of spongy bone","The osteon (Haversian system)","The epiphyseal plate","The medullary cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The osteon, or Haversian system, is the basic cylindrical structural unit of compact bone, built of concentric lamellae around a central canal. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Small cavities called lacunae within bone tissue house which cells?","choices":["Chondrocytes","Adipocytes","Osteocytes","Neurons"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lacunae are the small cavities in the bone matrix that house osteocytes, the mature bone cells. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"The epiphyseal (growth) plate is located in which region of a growing long bone?","choices":["Between the epiphysis and the diaphysis","Within the medullary cavity","At the very center of the diaphysis","Surrounding the outer periosteum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The epiphyseal plate is the cartilage zone located between the epiphysis (end) and the diaphysis (shaft) of a growing long bone. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How many vertebrae are typically present in the adult vertebral column?","choices":["26 vertebrae","33 vertebrae","24 vertebrae","30 vertebrae"],"answer":0,"exp":"The adult vertebral column typically has 26 vertebrae, because the sacral and coccygeal vertebrae have fused into the sacrum and coccyx. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How many cervical vertebrae are in the vertebral column?","choices":["7 cervical vertebrae","12 cervical vertebrae","5 cervical vertebrae","4 cervical vertebrae"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are 7 cervical vertebrae, forming the bony framework of the neck. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"How many pairs of ribs are found in the typical thoracic cage?","choices":["10 pairs of ribs","7 pairs of ribs","12 pairs of ribs","14 pairs of ribs"],"answer":2,"exp":"The thoracic cage typically contains 12 pairs of ribs, articulating with the 12 thoracic vertebrae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which process of the temporal bone joins the zygomatic bone to form the zygomatic arch?","choices":["The mastoid process","The zygomatic process","The styloid process","The pterygoid process"],"answer":1,"exp":"The zygomatic process of the temporal bone extends forward to meet the zygomatic bone, together forming the zygomatic arch of the cheek. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
